-- codebase's first-ever genuine QT-RC-07 attempt.
--
-- ============================================================
-- PASSAGE PORTFOLIO GAP ANALYSIS (Section 3 of this task's own
-- directive), AGAINST THE 3 EXISTING VALIDATED PASSAGES
-- ============================================================
-- "The Boat in the Boathouse" (migration 097): third-person, past
-- tense, SIBLING-PAIR protagonist structure (Priya + Kabir), practical/
-- restorative conflict (repairing an inherited boat), resolved via a
-- DISCOVERED OBJECT (a note). "The Understudy" (migration 152): third-
-- person, past tense, SINGLE-PROTAGONIST-plus-rival-turned-ally
-- structure (Maya + Ruby), internal/emotional conflict (self-doubt),
-- resolved via a RELATIONSHIP SHIFT. "How Bees Find Their Way Home"
-- (migration 152): present-tense, informational/popular-science,
-- SINGLE-TOPIC multi-method explanation (several ways one phenomenon —
-- navigation — works).
--
-- Every one of the 3 existing passages is third-person or non-
-- narrative; NONE is first-person. Every fiction passage uses a TWO-
-- character structure; NONE uses an ensemble (3+). Both fiction
-- passages resolve their conflict through a DISCOVERED OBJECT or a
-- quiet RELATIONSHIP SHIFT; NEITHER resolves through dialogue-driven,
-- time-pressured practical problem-solving. The one informational
-- passage explains ONE phenomenon via multiple methods; NONE compares
-- TWO named, contrasting entities. QT-RC-07 (which the taxonomy itself
-- defines as "two distinct characters... two separately-scored fields,
-- one per entity" — CSSE_QUESTION_INTELLIGENCE_FRAMEWORK.md, Primary
-- Competency RC-01, "Literal Retrieval from NARRATIVE Text") has zero
-- coverage anywhere in the estate.
--
-- ============================================================
-- PASSAGE A — "The Loose Connection" (eng-inc002-roboticsfinal)
-- ============================================================
-- Deliberately diversifies EVERY axis the gap analysis above named as
-- absent: FIRST-PERSON voice (new); ENSEMBLE protagonist structure, 3
-- named characters (narrator, Nisha, Ade — new); practical/technical
-- conflict under TIME PRESSURE (a competition robot's control wiring
-- fails 90 minutes before judging); resolved through DIALOGUE-DRIVEN,
-- METHOD-VS-ASSUMPTION problem-solving, not a discovered object (new);
-- NEW vocabulary domain (robotics/engineering: drivetrain, connector,
-- terminal — genuinely distinct from boatbuilding, drama, and biology).
-- Genuine transfer target, not cosmetic variation: this passage tests
-- whether a learner can track WHO did WHAT (two named characters'
-- separate, parallel troubleshooting actions) and reason about WHY a
-- careful, methodical approach succeeded where a confident, fast
-- assumption did not — a different reasoning pattern from either
-- existing fiction passage's own emotional-arc or restorative-project
-- structure.
--
-- QT-RC-07, AUTHENTICALLY GROUNDED, NOT MANUFACTURED: re-read directly
-- from CSSE_QUESTION_INTELLIGENCE_FRAMEWORK.md before authoring a
-- single word of this passage (Section 5 of this task's own directive):
-- the real evidence base (CSSE-003 Q8, "two unnamed men, two facts
-- each"; CSSE-008 Q15, "Inigo's and Mrs Tarvin's feelings about each
-- other") is CHARACTER-based and NARRATIVE-scoped, not a general two-
-- entity-comparison format — Primary Competency is explicitly "RC-01
-- (Literal Retrieval from Narrative Text)". An earlier draft of this
-- migration considered putting QT-RC-07 on Passage B (comparing sailing
-- ships and steamships) instead; that would have been exactly the
-- "manufactured to fill a coverage cell" risk this task's own directive
-- warned against, since informational-text entity comparison has never
-- been evidenced for this Question Type. Passage A's own two named,
-- co-present characters (Nisha, Ade), each independently attempting to
-- fix the same problem, is the genuinely authentic fit: Question 7 asks
-- what EACH of them did, as two separately-scored subparts (7a, 7b),
-- exactly matching the evidenced "two separately-scored fields, one per
-- entity" structural feature.
--
-- ============================================================
-- PASSAGE B — "Crossing the Atlantic: Sail and Steam" (eng-inc002-
-- sailandsteam)
-- ============================================================
-- Second informational passage, deliberately COMPARATIVE in structure
-- (unlike Bee Navigation's own single-topic/multi-method structure) and
-- in a wholly new subject domain (maritime/industrial history, not
-- biology) with new vocabulary (becalmed, drivetrain-adjacent engineering
-- terms are avoided here in favour of coal/boiler/timetable/reliability
-- vocabulary). Does NOT use QT-RC-07 (see above) — its own comparative
-- content is instead assessed through ordinary retrieval, judgement,
-- vocabulary, and word-choice-effect questions, all using already-
-- proven scoring tiers.
--
-- QT-RC-08, INVESTIGATED AND EXPLICITLY DEFERRED, NOT FORCED: this
-- migration's own first draft considered a QT-RC-08 (List-N-Items
-- Extraction) question for Passage B ("list two difficulties
-- steamships had"). Investigation of lib/learningEngine/
-- englishAnswerValidation.ts found no validation tier genuinely built
-- for this Question Type's own defined shape (a free-text answer
-- containing several independent, partially-creditable items) — TIER2_
-- ACCEPTED_SET scores a single phrase, not partial credit across
-- multiple required items; TIER5_NAMED_COMPONENT_PLUS_EXPLANATION's own
-- only real precedent (migration 044's w1-kitemaker-07) turns out to be
-- a single-answer "how does X feel" question mislabelled QT-RC-08, not
-- a genuine list-items question. Forcing this question into an ill-
-- fitting tier would have risked exactly the kind of defect Decision
-- 233 found and fixed for Continuous Writing's own data-contract
-- mismatch. QT-RC-08 is therefore explicitly deferred, not attempted —
-- Passage B's seventh question uses QT-RC-10 instead, a well-proven
-- tier, on genuinely different content (why the writer disclosed
-- steam's own risks, not just its advantages).
--
-- ============================================================
-- FACTUAL VERIFICATION CONTROL (Passage B only; Passage A is fiction,
-- no real-world factual claims)
-- ============================================================
-- Two real-world claims, both independently verified via WebSearch this
-- session across multiple mutually-consistent, authoritative sources
-- (Wikipedia "SS Great Western"; Britannica "Great Western (ship)";
-- History Skills "What made the SS Great Western the first Atlantic
-- steamship"; The Geography of Transport Systems, "Liner Transatlantic
-- Crossing Times, 1833-1952"):
--   (1) "In 1838... she travelled from Bristol... to New York in about
--       fifteen days." SOURCE-CONTAINS: the Great Western's maiden
--       voyage departed Bristol 8 April 1838 and reached New York in
--       15 (some sources: 15.5) days; regular service over 1838-1840
--       averaged about 16 days westbound. ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: "about
--       fifteen days" is used as a single, clean, memorable figure
--       rather than distinguishing the maiden voyage from the regular-
--       service average, or westbound from eastbound (13 days), which
--       would overload a 10-11-year-old reader with near-duplicate
--       numbers. FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH (multiple independent,
--       mutually-consistent sources). UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: none
--       for the figure itself.
--   (2) "roughly half the time a sailing ship usually needed" / sailing
--       crossings "usually took about a month [and] could sometimes
--       stretch on for six weeks or more." SOURCE-CONTAINS: The
--       Geography of Transport Systems' own liner-crossing-times data:
--       contemporary sailing packet ships averaged roughly 33 days
--       westbound and 22 days eastbound in this era. ANGEL-
--       SIMPLIFICATION: rounded to "about a month" (westbound, the
--       direction this passage's own comparison uses) with "six weeks
--       or more" as the disclosed, genuinely-occurring upper bound when
--       becalmed, rather than citing the precise 33-day mean.
--       FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH. UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: none for
--       the comparison itself.
-- DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED: this passage never asserts the Great
-- Western was "the first" steamship to cross the Atlantic in any
-- absolute sense — a genuinely contested historical footnote (the
-- Sirius technically arrived in New York about a day earlier in April
-- 1838, though she was a coastal packet steamer pressed into an
-- opportunistic crossing, not purpose-built for the Atlantic route).
-- The passage instead states only that the Great Western was "one of
-- the first steamships built specially to cross the Atlantic" —
-- accurate and uncontested — and this footnote is sidestepped by
-- design, per this task's own explicit "if a claim is contested,
-- express the uncertainty accurately, remove the claim, or choose a
-- better-supported subject" instruction: here, removing the "first"
-- claim entirely, since the pedagogical content (the TIME comparison)
-- does not depend on it.
--
