('eng-inc002-sailandsteam-q07', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'hard', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc002-sailandsteam-q07","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"The writer chose to explain that running out of coal was 'a genuine danger early engineers took very seriously', rather than only describing the advantages of steam power. Why might the writer have included this detail?","modelAnswer":"It shows that steam power was not simply a straightforward improvement over sailing -- it also introduced new dangers and responsibilities. Including this detail makes the passage feel more balanced and honest about steam power's real trade-offs, rather than only praising it.","passageTitle":"Crossing the Atlantic: Sail and Steam","passageText":"For hundreds of years, the fastest way to cross the Atlantic Ocean was by sailing ship, and for just as long, \"fast\" meant something very different from what it means today. A sailing ship depends entirely on the wind: when the wind blows strongly from a helpful direction, a well-built ship can make good progress, but when it drops away completely, the ship can sit almost motionless for days, waiting. Sailors called this being becalmed, and a becalmed ship could do nothing but wait for the weather to change. Because of this, a crossing that usually took about a month could sometimes stretch on for six weeks or more, and nobody setting out could say, with any real confidence, exactly when they would arrive.\n\nSteam power changed this completely. Instead of relying on wind pushing against sails, a steamship burned coal to heat water into steam, and the steam drove an engine that turned paddle wheels or, later, a propeller, pushing the ship forward regardless of which way the wind happened to be blowing. In 1838, a ship called the Great Western became one of the first steamships built specially to cross the Atlantic, rather than being adapted from a ship designed for shorter journeys. On her very first voyage, she travelled from Bristol, in England, to New York in about fifteen days, roughly half the time a sailing ship usually needed for the same crossing.\n\nThis difference mattered enormously to the people making the journey. A passenger boarding a sailing packet ship had to prepare for weeks at sea with no way of knowing whether the crossing would take four weeks or six; letters, cargo, and passengers alike were, in a very real sense, at the mercy of the weather. A passenger boarding a steamship like the Great Western could expect something closer to a fixed timetable, since the engine did not care whether the wind was blowing or not. This predictability was, in many ways, just as important as the extra speed: shipping companies could begin to promise a departure date and an arrival date, and reasonably expect to keep to both.\n\nSteam crossings were not without their own difficulties, however. A steamship needed to carry an enormous quantity of coal, since every day at sea meant more coal burned, and running out partway across the ocean was a genuine danger early engineers took very seriously. Steam engines also required constant attention from a crew of engineers, working in hot, noisy conditions below deck, quite unlike the comparatively quiet work of adjusting sails above deck on a sailing ship. For some years after 1838, sailing ships and steamships shared the ocean, each suited to slightly different journeys and different kinds of cargo, before steam power gradually became the standard for carrying passengers across the Atlantic.\n\nWhat the story of the Great Western shows, above all, is that being faster was not the only advantage steam power offered. Reliability, the simple ability to know roughly when a ship would arrive, turned out to matter just as much as how quickly it could get there.","acceptedAnswers":["it shows steam power had real risks/downsides too, not just advantages","it makes the passage feel more balanced/fair, not one-sided","it shows engineers had to manage a genuine new danger, not just enjoy the benefits","it stops the passage from sounding like it is only praising steam power"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 002 (Decision 237). QT-RC-10, competency RC-02, passage "Crossing the Atlantic: Sail and Steam". Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3, matching the estate''s own established open ''why might the writer'' word-choice-effect format. Used here in place of an originally-considered QT-RC-08 question -- see this migration''s own header for why QT-RC-08 was deferred rather than forced into an unproven scoring tier.', 2, 'eng-inc002-sailandsteam',
